## Notes

- `--non-interactive` and `--yes` together suppress all prompts ÔÇö use both in CI.
- A short flag means one thing on every command: `-y` is `--yes` wherever `--yes` exists, and `-a` is 
`--architecture`. `unity projects list` and `unity projects size` spell "every project" `--all`; write that, not `-a`. 
An old spelling the CLI still accepts (`-a` there, `--project` on `status`, `--template-version`, `--hub-version`, 
`--package-version`, `--list-components`, `--install-dir`, `--childModules`, `--wait-timeout`, `--git-token-stdin`, 
`--group_by` on `command`, `--local-space` on `collaboration annotations`, `--project-id` and `--organization-id` on 
`collaboration`, `-v` on `projects list`, `--since` on `releases` and `vcs git migrate-lfs`) prints a warning naming 
the spelling to use; so does `--verbose` on `editors` and `projects list`, which still shows every column but now 
spells it `--long`; in `--format json` it is in the envelope's `warnings`. Use the new spelling, so the warning never 
lands in a log you parse.
- `--timeout` takes a whole number of seconds written as digits (`--timeout 600`) on every command, and so do 
`UNITY_BUILD_TIMEOUT`, `UNITY_RUN_TIMEOUT` and `UNITY_TEST_TIMEOUT`. A unit, fraction or exponent (`30s`, `5m`, `1.5`, 
`1e3`) is a usage error (exit `2`), so convert minutes to seconds yourself.
- `--format json` always produces machine-readable output; prefer it over parsing human text. Error envelopes are 
pretty-printed with the same 2-space indent as success envelopes.
- **`success` is `true` exactly when the exit code is `0`.** A status command whose answer is no (`unity auth status` 
signed out, `unity license status` with no license, `unity status` with no ready Editor) is a failure: `success: 
false`, an `errors[0].code` naming the answer, and its report still in `data`. A listing that finds nothing (`unity 
editors running` with no Editor) succeeds with an empty list.
- **Read failures from stdout, not stderr.** A failed command still writes a complete document to stdout: under 
`--format json` an envelope with `success: false` and a populated `errors` array (`errors[0].code` is the stable token 
to branch on); under `--format ndjson` the usual terminal `{"type":"result","success":false,ÔÇª}` frame. **Every 
ndjson stream ends with exactly one terminal `{"type":"result"}` frame, on success too**, so a stream without one was 
truncated. Table-shaped listings (`unity editors`, `unity editors upgrade --check`, `unity templates list`, `unity 
releases`, `unity projects list`, `unity context list`, `unity auth list`, `unity modules list`, `unity list`, `unity 
doctor`, `unity cloud org list`, `unity cloud project list`) write one row per line first, with no `type` field on the 
rows, then a terminal frame whose `data` is `{"count": N}`. A single-record answer (`unity version`, `unity language`, 
`unity config proxy`, `unity cache info` and the like) is that frame alone, its `data` the same as `--format json`; 
`unity env` writes its rows, then the frame. `--quiet` never changes json output. It never changes ndjson output 
either, except that `unity editors upgrade`, `unity editors module list`, `unity install-modules`, `unity build 
--list-targets`, `unity build --list-profiles` and `unity env` still drop their rows under `--quiet` (the terminal 
frame stays), a known bug; leave `--quiet` off when you need those rows. A warning in either format appears only in 
`warnings`, never on stderr. A `--watch` listing, which only ends on Ctrl-C, writes no terminal frame. A few commands 
(`unity install-modules --list` among them) still end a successful ndjson stream without one, which is a known bug. 
**Branch on `success`, never on `data`** ÔÇö `data` is usually `null` on a failure, but not always: a partial `unity 
editors add` failure carries a row per path, and an ambiguous `unity auth switch` carries `data.candidates` for you to 
disambiguate with. Check `success` and the exit code ÔÇö never treat empty stdout as a failure signal, and do not 
parse stderr, which carries only human diagnostics in these formats. If stdout is empty on a non-zero exit, that is a 
known bug in that command rather than a shape you should code against.
- `unity <version> [path]` is a shorthand for `unity open [path] --editor-version <version>`. Works with `lts`, 
`latest`, or a full version string like `6000.0.47f1`.
- The CLI supports kubectl-style plugins: any `unity-<name>` binary on PATH is callable as `unity <name>`.
- Terminal output is hardened against control-character / escape-sequence injection from server-provided values 
(project titles, editor versions, module names) ÔÇö C0 controls and non-SGR escape sequences are stripped from 
table/list/tree output, and now also from Commander usage errors, the `unity bug` log-archive warning, and `unity 
projects add`/`remove` machine (tsv) output, while SGR color/style codes are preserved.
- The CLI reports anonymous crashes and errors via Sentry to help fix bugs (no IP address or hostname; home-directory 
paths and token-like values scrubbed before send), aligned with the Unity Hub. Opting in to analytics additionally 
attaches an anonymized machine id; opted-out users stay fully anonymous. Set `UNITY_NO_CRASH_REPORT` to disable 
reporting entirely. Separately again, every run sends one anonymous `cli telemetry` usage ping regardless of 
analytics/consent state ÔÇö see 
[diagnostics-maintenance.md](references/diagnostics-maintenance.md#analytics--usagetelemetry-consent).
- `unity --version` prints the version you have. `unity self-update --check --format json` says whether a newer one 
exists on your installed channel (a beta follows betas). `unity self-update --check --channel stable --format json` 
checks for a stable release, for example when moving off a beta once GA ships.
- **Always use the latest version of the Unity CLI unless you are told to use a specific version.** A newer CLI 
regularly adds commands and output fields these docs describe, so an outdated one fails in ways that read like the 
docs being wrong.
- As of `0.1.0-beta.8` the CLI checks in the background for a newer version and prints an unobtrusive "update 
available" notice (interactive sessions only; never delays a command). Turn it off with `unity config update-check 
off` or the `UNITY_NO_UPDATE_CHECK` env var.
- The same check also covers plugins the CLI installed (`unity plugin install`): when one has a newer version, a 
notice names it and suggests `unity plugin upgrade`, at most once a day. The same two switches turn it off.
- **Under `--format json` and `--format ndjson` that same notice reaches you as a `notifications` array on the 
envelope** ÔÇö see [Notifications](#notifications) below. Over MCP it arrives once per session, as prose in the 
server's `initialize` `instructions`.
- Outbound HTTP from every CLI command honors the resolved proxy (see `unity config proxy`). An invalid `--proxy` 
value (malformed URL or unsupported scheme) fails with a usage error (exit 2) instead of being silently ignored. 
Inspect what the CLI actually resolved with `unity env --format json` or `unity doctor --format json` ÔÇö both surface 
the active proxy URL, its source, and auth source.

